00:17:03.944 --> 00:17:05.536
You were warned. Let's go.


00:17:06.624 --> 00:17:08.535
I thought you said
the target was Romanov's.


00:17:08.584 --> 00:17:11.462
- This is research.
- Research?


00:17:11.504 --> 00:17:12.983
Oh, wow:


00:17:18.144 --> 00:17:20.135
They're absolutely stunning.


00:17:22.784 --> 00:17:24.661
They are.


00:17:24.704 --> 00:17:27.457
Between 1885 and 1916,


00:17:27.504 --> 00:17:30.655
Fabergé produced 50
of these Easter eggs for the Empress


00:17:30.704 --> 00:17:33.172
and her daughter-in -law,
the Czarina Alexandra.


00:17:33.224 --> 00:17:35.055
Should I take notes?


00:17:35.104 --> 00:17:36.935
Will there be a quiz?


00:17:36.984 --> 00:17:40.101
No quiz but there will be a test.


00:17:40.144 --> 00:17:45.537
OK. The Fabergés are all made
of precious metals and fine jewels.


00:17:45.584 --> 00:17:49.213
- Every one a unique masterpiece.
- Indeed.


00:17:49.264 --> 00:17:51.494
Most are on display in the Kremlin.


00:17:51.544 --> 00:17:55.219
The rest in private collections,
museums or lost.


00:17:55.264 --> 00:17:57.653
In this case, at Romanov's.


00:17:58.624 --> 00:18:00.660
Who is your buyer?


00:18:00.704 --> 00:18:02.296
You don't want to know.


00:18:02.344 --> 00:18:05.495
Oh, I'm stealing $40 million worth
of eggs so I want to know.


00:18:05.544 --> 00:18:07.978
I also want to know
why these particular eggs


00:18:08.024 --> 00:18:09.616
are even worth $40 million.


00:18:10.984 --> 00:18:13.418
Because no one knows they exist.


00:18:14.224 --> 00:18:15.543
Oh.


00:18:15.584 --> 00:18:18.496
- The so-called mystery eggs?
- Mm-hm.


00:18:18.544 --> 00:18:22.457
Yeah, I've heard rumours,
but I thought it was just a myth.


00:18:22.504 --> 00:18:26.975
Last year, they turned up.
At Romanov's.


00:18:27.024 --> 00:18:28.776
So how do you know this?


00:18:28.824 --> 00:18:33.420
I was partners with a man named
Victor Korolenko. Alex's father.


00:18:33.464 --> 00:18:35.216
He was in tight with the Russians.


00:18:35.264 --> 00:18:37.937
What they knew, he knew.


00:18:37.984 --> 00:18:40.623
So why aren't you
pulling this job with him?


00:18:40.664 --> 00:18:43.337
Let's just say he's no longer around.


00:18:43.384 --> 00:18:45.534
Leave it at that.


00:18:49.544 --> 00:18:51.057
He's gone.


00:18:51.104 --> 00:18:52.583
You're here.


00:18:54.184 --> 00:18:55.776
Now we just have to deliver.


00:18:58.424 --> 00:19:00.779
- Ripley...
- Hmm?


00:19:00.824 --> 00:19:01.813
Why?


00:19:03.304 --> 00:19:04.783
Why risk your freedom at all?


00:19:05.864 --> 00:19:07.855
This will not be
anything close to easy.


00:19:07.904 --> 00:19:09.815
You are not in jail in the present.


00:19:09.864 --> 00:19:13.174
- Are you not comfortable?
- I'm never comfortable.


00:19:14.304 --> 00:19:16.977
Some people
were born to compose music.


00:19:18.304 --> 00:19:20.056
Others to split the atom.


00:19:22.864 --> 00:19:25.094
I was born to steal shit.


00:19:27.384 --> 00:19:29.022
Hold out your hand.


00:19:30.504 --> 00:19:31.573
Hand?


00:19:36.584 --> 00:19:40.293
That coin was given to me by Victor
when we became partners.


00:19:40.344 --> 00:19:42.778
The coin represents
a Russian phrase.


00:19:42.824 --> 00:19:47.944
<i>Vorovoslky mlr
Loosely translated, thieves' world.</i>


00:19:48.984 --> 00:19:50.337
Victor belonged to it.


00:19:50.384 --> 00:19:51.612
Y owww


00:19:52.624 --> 00:19:54.501
you belong to it.


00:20:31.624 --> 00:20:33.819
Psst. Hey:


00:20:33.864 --> 00:20:37.459
You misunderstand.
Ripley's just business.


00:20:37.504 --> 00:20:39.301
This is...


00:20:39.344 --> 00:20:40.743
Stalking?


00:20:42.184 --> 00:20:45.494
Well, if you had given me your number,
I would have called first.


00:20:46.824 --> 00:20:49.702
Those are two very flawed assumptions.


00:20:49.744 --> 00:20:51.302
Do you have a vase?


00:20:51.344 --> 00:20:53.221
I just took these
from a roadside shrine


00:20:53.264 --> 00:20:55.983
and I think they need some water.


00:20:57.944 --> 00:21:01.778
May I use that... thing over there?
Yeah, yeah.


00:21:02.784 --> 00:21:08.336
I've noticed this Russian nightclub
down the block.


00:21:08.384 --> 00:21:10.261
Riga Rose or something like that.


00:21:10.304 --> 00:21:12.340
Why don't we meet there after work?


00:21:17.864 --> 00:21:19.092
I'm busy.


00:21:20.064 --> 00:21:23.261
- I'll wait.
- I won't show.


00:21:23.304 --> 00:21:25.101
I'll take my chances.


00:21:28.464 --> 00:21:31.376
<i>Do svldanja, Alexandra.</i>


00:21:32.104 --> 00:21:36.336
Research. Keith Ripley.
'93, the Brooklyn Museum.


00:21:36.384 --> 00:21:39.182
'94, the Diamond District Warehouse
and then in '95...


00:21:39.224 --> 00:21:42.500
I'm very familiar
with Ripley's life story, Michaels.


00:21:42.544 --> 00:21:45.775
In fact, I busted him twice.


00:21:45.824 --> 00:21:48.258
Both times he walked
because of good lawyering.


00:21:48.304 --> 00:21:51.137
Exactly, so I thought
why not swing for the fences?


00:21:51.184 --> 00:21:53.015
Murder one, Victor Korolenko.


00:21:53.064 --> 00:21:55.862
Thinkin ' of the Brighton Beach steam bath?
Forget it. There was no...


00:21:55.904 --> 00:21:58.737
- No body. But we've got a ton of forensics.
- And no witnesses.


00:21:58.784 --> 00:22:01.696
Nobody there admitted
to seeing a thing.


00:22:01.744 --> 00:22:05.100
I was this close to convincing
Korolenko to take witness protection,


00:22:05.144 --> 00:22:07.339
in exchange for giving up Ripley.


00:22:07.384 --> 00:22:09.852
But Ripley got to him first.


00:22:09.904 --> 00:22:11.462
I can't prove it.


00:22:11.504 --> 00:22:13.654
Right, OK. I see, I see. OK.


00:22:13.704 --> 00:22:16.582
No, what you don't see, Michaels,


00:22:16.624 --> 00:22:19.457
is why Ripley got involved
in a small-time caper,


00:22:19.504 --> 00:22:22.621
like grabbin ' a few diamonds
off of some couriers on a subway.


00:22:22.664 --> 00:22:23.699
And neither did I.


00:22:23.744 --> 00:22:26.019
Until I read
the Dutch guy's statement.


00:22:28.024 --> 00:22:29.935
Those diamonds came from Romanov's.


00:22:31.344 --> 00:22:34.859
You mean Ripley and his new partner
are planning to take down Romanov's?


00:22:36.024 --> 00:22:38.584
Ah, come on:
Like that could ever happen:


00:22:38.624 --> 00:22:41.422
Not even Ripley
could take down a place like that.


00:22:42.624 --> 00:22:43.613
Wanna bet?


00:23:10.264 --> 00:23:12.300
- Hi.
- Hi.


00:23:12.344 --> 00:23:15.700
- You know this place very well.
- I never said I didn't.


00:23:15.744 --> 00:23:17.894
Let's face it,
you didn't see the nightclub.


00:23:17.944 --> 00:23:21.539
You saw the flyer in the fax
